<ManualLayout
  slug="the-5620"
  title="The DMD 5620"
  description="The bitmapped terminal with its own processor: what it is, its two screen sizes, the phosphor, and why it costs CPU when nothing is happening."
>
  <h2>A computer that happens to have a screen</h2>
  <p>
    The Blit — sold by Teletype as the DMD 5620 — was designed by Bart Locanthi at Bell Labs and
    is the reason Eighth Edition looks like anything at all. It has its own 32-bit processor, a
    megabyte of memory, and an 800×1024 one-bit display, and it connects to the host over a single
    serial line.
  </p>
  <p>
    The important part is what that made possible: programs could be <em>downloaded into the
    terminal</em> and run there. The host sends a binary down the wire, the terminal executes it,
    and from then on the two are cooperating processes on two different computers. That is what
    <a href={href('/manual/mux-and-layers')}><code>mux</code></a> is.
  </p>

  <h2>The two screens</h2>
  <p>
    ipnx offers two sizes, in <strong>Settings → Screen</strong>:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Preset</th><th>Pixels</th><th>Text</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Original</td><td>800 × 1024</td><td>88 columns</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Wide</td><td>1152 × 1024</td><td>127 columns</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Original is the machine as it shipped. Wide is not — it is a real modification to the
    terminal's firmware data, made at runtime, and it is included because a portrait CRT on a
    modern display leaves a great deal of desk unused.
  </p>
  <p>
    The window is locked to the shape of the screen rather than the other way round. You can scale
    it, but you cannot reshape it, because the terminal has a fixed number of pixels and pretending
    otherwise would just stretch them.
  </p>
  <p>
    One consequence worth knowing: on the Wide screen, <code>mux</code> needs a differently-built
    window system, and the login profile arranges that for you. If you ever run the window system
    by an explicit path and get a blank screen on Wide, that is why.
  </p>

  <h2>Phosphor</h2>
  <p>
    A one-bit display has no colours, only lit and unlit, so the colour of "lit" is yours to
    choose — green, amber or white, in Settings. It is a tint applied to the emulated raster, not a
    filter over the app, so it affects nothing else.
  </p>

  <h2>Scaling, and the moiré</h2>
  <p>
    Eighth Edition's window system draws with stipple patterns — alternating pixels — for grey.
    At a fractional zoom those patterns beat against the display grid and crawl unpleasantly.
    <strong>Crisp</strong> scaling fixes it by rounding to a whole-number multiple, so one terminal
    pixel is exactly two or three screen pixels. <strong>Fill</strong> uses the whole window and
    accepts the shimmer.
  </p>
  <p>
    Crisp cannot always be honoured — below 1:1 there is no whole number to round to — so in a very
    small window it falls back to filling.
  </p>

  <h2>Why it uses CPU when nothing is happening</h2>
  <p>
    Because a real 5620 did. The VAX idles properly: its kernel executes a wait instruction and the
    simulator sleeps, so a machine sitting at a login prompt costs almost nothing. The terminal has
    no such instruction — its processor genuinely spins, polling its keyboard and its serial port —
    and emulating that faithfully means emulating the spin.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Closing the 5620 window stops it.</strong> The thread ends and the core comes back. If
    you are working in the glass terminals and want the fans quiet, close the terminal you are not
    using.
  </p>

  <h2>Its memory survives</h2>
  <p>
    The 5620 had non-volatile memory for its settings, and so does this one — baud rate and the
    rest persist between launches. So does the screen: what was last drawn is painted back when you
    resume, so a restored session looks like the one you left rather than a blank tube.
  </p>
</ManualLayout>
